8 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1045 "The Unit Step Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152368 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a751e90e-5582-5860-bc58-1abf98d12fb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1061 23719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d46b30d-17ce-5fe6-a7be-dcb1352a6151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1061 23720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70b202ae-ec41-5eae-8ae1-a2c1f7937280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1061 23721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2968451-7379-5754-b957-4337d8ab3c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1061 23722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d98898-5aa7-5f1a-9c11-0b4e8416b8d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153285 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153285 #edb/ref 1061 594596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153285 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "455e69f6-8055-57cd-bf38-cc55f0003b38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153285 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153286 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153286 #edb/ref 1061 594597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6fb18f8f-9225-59d6-ba9d-420587429cd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153287 #edb/ref 1061 594601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3465d29d-4b4b-54cf-a0d9-78eb104b93b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153288 #edb/ref 1061 594605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a775d0ca-e058-5b27-861e-06ae54541b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153289 #edb/ref 1061 594606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3412e7e4-2573-5dc3-9a83-7384d8fcdbcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153296 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153296 #edb/ref 1061 606622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a133e08-5708-5982-93e2-5eab7d7c7ec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153326 #edb/ref 1064 "The **unit step function**, $u(t)$, is used to model quantities that change suddenly at a specific time, such as the application of a force, the switching of a circuit, or the start of a signal.\n\nThe unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1, & t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the function is *off* before $t=0$ and *on* afterward. The graph of the unit step function is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nThere are two primary ways to modify the unit step function:\n\n- **Scaling**: Multiplying $u(t)$ by a constant changes the magnitude (height) of the step.\n\n- **Shifting**: Replacing $t$ with $t-a$ changes the turn-on time from $t=0$ to $t=a.$ Applying only the shift results in the function $u(t - a),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0, & t<a, \\\\ 1, & t\\ge a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function remains at $0$ until $t=a$ and then immediately jumps to $1.$\n\nCombining both modifications gives the general form of a **scaled and shifted** step function:\n\n$$\nf(t) = c \\cdot u(t-a)\n$$\n\nThis function remains at $0$ for $t < a$, and then jumps to the constant value $c$ at $t=a.$ In this way, the unit step function allows us to model both the *timing* and the *magnitude* of a sudden change.\n\nNext, we will look at a concrete example of graphing a specific scaled and shifted step function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153326 #edb/ref 1065 14428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1ad92e0c-c4dc-56b8-844e-8ab91997f653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153326 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153327 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have seen how scaling and shifting affect the unit step function.\nWe now examine how the unit step function behaves under **reflection**.\n\nThe graph of $y = u(-t)$ represents the reflection of the unit step function $u(t)$ across the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/d0/d015f0a844d7434732331b6aae46cfda599814b76a48ee06b8af17f0918acca3.png)\n\nUnder this transformation, the step still occurs at $t = 0$, but the direction of the step is reversed. In particular,\n\n- $u(t)$ *turns on* at $t=0$ and remains on for $t>0,$\n\n- $u(-t)$ *turns off* at $t=0$ and remains off for $t>0.$\n\nNow, let’s look at a few examples to see how reflection works in practice." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153327 #edb/ref 1065 14429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153327 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "acce83df-ebbe-5fd6-9a28-25e74dc1180c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153327 #edb/ref 1067 "The Function y = u(-t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153336 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider an example of a scaled and shifted step function $c\\cdot u(t-a).$ \n\nLet $f(t) = -2u(t-3),$ where $u$ is the unit step function. We evaluate this function at $t=2.$\n\n Substituting $t = 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2u(2-3) \\\\  & =-2u(-1) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(2)=0.$\n\nIn the graph, $f(2)$ lies on the flat blue segment before the step activates, so its value is $0.$\n\n![](images/29/29b53bd2128d4586edcde3e814bd10614948e02dbd082d5dfbf78efff2ae8ca5.png)\n\nNow, let’s get some practice working through a few examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153336 #edb/ref 1065 14858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153336 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28b901e3-9da2-5dfa-a9ca-cab79599d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153336 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1065 3987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186155542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc82df55-72c6-562d-ac48-86abb6081419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155542 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "898de07e-22f0-5b81-a6cd-37d35343a1b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155543 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155543 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155543 #edb/ref 108